{"task": "inspect_evals/stereoset", "action": "not_counted", "treatment": "scores behaviour, not answer correctness", "reason": "Scores stereotype preference, +1 for the stereotype and -1 for the anti-stereotype, and its accuracy scorer marks both of those options correct. A preference between two answers that both count as right is behaviour, not answer correctness.", "source": "inspect_evals 0.20.0, src/inspect_evals/stereoset/stereoset.py:215 label_to_score = {\"stereotype\": 1, \"anti-stereotype\": -1, \"unrelated\": 0}; :245-246 marks both stereotypical and anti-stereotypical options as correct", "date": "2026-10-10", "author": "ebt55"}
# The note below was added on 2026-10-10, after the 0.24.0 close-out sweep, for its
# one non-tie flag. It classifies the flag and moves none (NOTES.md, 2026-10-10).
{"task": "inspect_evals/bbh", "action": "note", "reason": "Task property, recorded after the 0.24.0 sweep; it moves no flag. Every bbh input carries its subset's few-shot examples, each ending in an ANSWER line, and the scorers read the last one, so echo answers each subset with that subset's last example label: a constant guess. It clears the dataset-wide majority (B, 1030 of 6508) because B is no answer on the six binary subsets, where echo scores 697; on the multiple-choice subsets it is below majority (990 vs 1030). It never beats a subset's own most common answer except on word_sorting, where 8 inputs list their words already sorted. Lines are inspect_evals 0.24.0 (bbh.py) and inspect_ai 0.3.263 (the two parsers).", "source": "its few-shot examples end in `ANSWER: <label>` (bbh.py:98-108) and the scorers keep the last `ANSWER:` (_multiple_choice.py:106-115, _util/pattern.py:1-8), so echo gives one fixed label per subset: a property of the task, not a loose scorer", "date": "2026-10-10", "author": "ebt55"}
